<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext policy for RELEASE builds. The debug source set overrides this whole
  file (see src/debug/res/xml/network_security_config.xml).

  Why a config rather than the `usesCleartextTraffic` manifest flag: on API 24+ —
  and minSdk here is 24 — `networkSecurityConfig` SUPERSEDES that flag entirely,
  so the two cannot be mixed. The flag stays in the manifest for documentation
  and is inert.

  The one exception below is our own loopback. The RFC 8252 sign-in binds a
  listener on 127.0.0.1:<random port> and the gateway redirects the WebView to it
  (`src-tauri/src/oauth.rs`); with cleartext refused, that redirect fails and the
  sign-in dies holding an authorization code it can never deliver. The redirect
  URI cannot be https — it terminates inside this process, there is no
  certificate to present — and it cannot be a custom scheme either, because the
  gateway validates it as a loopback IP literal
  (`_validate_loopback_redirect_uri` in hermes_cli/dashboard_auth/routes.py).

  Scoped to the loopback addresses and nothing else: traffic that never leaves the
  device is not the thing cleartext policy exists to protect. A plain-http GATEWAY
  on a LAN or Tailscale address is still refused in release, which is the intended
  posture — that is a real network hop.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">::1</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
